@index INDEX

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node MAIN
@next PLAY

@{c}@{h1}golem-next

@{c}MIDI for the ZX Spectrum Next: a Golem or any MIDI synth

@{h2}Commands

 @{" .golem play " LINK PLAY}        Play a MIDI file
 @{" .golem note " LINK NOTE}        Play a test note
 @{" .golem status " LINK STATUS}      Driver and Golem state
 @{" .golem engine " LINK ENGINE}      MT-32, FluidSynth or OPL3
 @{" .golem rom " LINK ROM}         MT-32 ROM set
 @{" .golem soundfont " LINK SOUNDFONT}   FluidSynth SoundFont
 @{" .golem bank " LINK BANK}        OPL3 instrument bank
 @{" .golem set " LINK SET}         Volume, effects, channels, keys...
 @{" .golem save " LINK SAVE}        Keep the settings after switching off
 @{" .golem output " LINK OUTPUT}      Golem or a joystick port
 @{" .golem usb " LINK USB}         USB MIDI devices on the Pi
 @{" .golem reset " LINK RESET}       Synth back to its factory state
 @{" .mt32, .gm, .opl3 " LINK ALIASES}  The same, choosing the engine first

@{h2}More

 @{" Playing a MIDI keyboard " LINK KEYBOARD}
 @{" Error messages " LINK ERRORS}
 @{" Where to find more " LINK MORE}

@{i}Right/Left@{ui} choose a link, @{i}ENTER@{ui} follows it, @{i}B@{ui} goes back, @{i}Q@{ui} quits.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node PLAY
@next NOTE
@prev MAIN
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem play

    @{b}.golem play song.mid@{ub}
    @{b}.golem play "my song.mid"@{ub}
    @{b}.opl3 play song.mid 58@{ub}

Plays a standard MIDI file: format 0 or 1, up to 24 tracks and 1 MB.
@{i}SPACE@{ui} stops it. A number after the file chooses the OPL3 bank first,
as @{" .golem bank " LINK BANK} does.

Before each song @{b}play@{ub} resets the synth (like @{" .golem reset " LINK RESET}), so
the previous song's SysEx does not carry over, releases the sustain pedal,
stops the notes and sets every channel's volume to 100. At the end it stops
all notes again.

The settings of @{" .golem set " LINK SET} survive that reset.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node NOTE
@next STATUS
@prev PLAY
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem note

    @{b}.golem note <note> <velocity> <seconds> [channel]@{ub}
    @{b}.golem note 60 100 2@{ub}

Plays one test note: note 0-127, velocity 1-127, 1-60 seconds and channel
1-16 (1 if omitted). It does not reset the synth.

With the MT-32 engine channel 1 is silent unless the channels are
@{" alternate " LINK SET}: use 2-10.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node STATUS
@next ENGINE
@prev NOTE
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem status

    @{b}.golem status@{ub}

Checks the driver and asks the Golem for its engine, ROM set and SoundFont:

    Golem: driver OK; session free; TX empty
    Golem: output Golem (Accel. header)
    Golem: engine MT32; ROM new; SoundFont 0

With the OPL3 engine it also gives its bank: @{b}engine OPL3; bank 72; ...@{ub}

"no answer" means the Pi did not reply within 0.2 seconds: it is off, still
starting, runs other firmware, or the output is an external synth.
"RX errors" means bytes arrived with errors.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node ENGINE
@next ROM
@prev STATUS
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem engine

    @{b}.golem engine mt32@{ub}
    @{b}.golem engine fluidsynth@{ub}
    @{b}.golem engine opl3@{ub}

Switches the Golem's engine: MT-32 (Munt, for Sierra, LucasArts and other
MT-32 music), FluidSynth (General MIDI with a SoundFont) or OPL3 (General
MIDI with FM synthesis, the sound of a Sound Blaster 16 or AdLib; its
instrument bank is chosen with @{" .golem bank " LINK BANK}).

It waits until the Pi confirms the change, up to about 30 seconds.
@{i}SPACE@{ui} ends the wait, but the Pi may still apply the change.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node ROM
@next SOUNDFONT
@prev ENGINE
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem rom

    @{b}.golem rom old@{ub}       MT-32 with version 1 ROMs
    @{b}.golem rom new@{ub}       MT-32 with version 2 ROMs
    @{b}.golem rom cm32l@{ub}     CM-32L

The ROMs must be on the Pi's SD card, in the @{i}roms@{ui} folder.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node SOUNDFONT
@next BANK
@prev ROM
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem soundfont

    @{b}.golem soundfont <0-127>@{ub}

Selects the FluidSynth SoundFont by number, in the order of the Pi's
@{i}soundfonts@{ui} folder. It also works with the MT-32 engine selected: it
prepares that SoundFont for the next @{b}engine fluidsynth@{ub}.

Loading a SoundFont takes time. @{b}Do not play music while it loads:@{ub} the
Pi does not serve the port meanwhile and could lose data.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node BANK
@next SET
@prev SOUNDFONT
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem bank

    @{b}.golem bank <0-127>@{ub}

Instrument bank of the OPL3 engine (72 at start-up). 0-78 are built in; they
come from games and sound drivers of the 1990s. Some to try: 0 AIL (The Fat
Man), 58 The Fat Man for Windows 9x, 59 The Fat Man 4-operator, 72 DMXOPL3.
Your own banks: WOPL files (made with OPL3 Bank Editor) in the @{i}opl3@{ui}
folder of the Pi's SD card are 79 onwards, in alphabetical order.
Changing the bank stops the notes and returns every channel to its first
instrument. Needs firmware golem-1.2.0.
Without a number it only reports the bank in use.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node SET
@next SAVE
@prev BANK
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem set

    @{b}.golem set volume <0-100>@{ub}
    @{b}.golem set fx on|off@{ub}
    @{b}.golem set channels std|alt@{ub}
    @{b}.golem set thru on|off@{ub}
    @{b}.golem set keys on|off@{ub}
    @{b}.golem set loud on|off@{ub}

Changes a setting in the Golem firmware. Without a value it only reports it,
for example @{b}.golem set volume@{ub} answers "Golem: volume 80".

@{h2}volume
Master volume of both engines, 0-100 (100 at start-up). The same one that
the buttons and the encoder of a Pi HAT change.

@{h2}fx
FluidSynth reverb and chorus for every channel. @{b}off@{ub} gives a dry sound,
for example for a piano played from a keyboard. @{b}on@{ub} turns them back on
with the levels of mt32-pi.cfg. It lasts across SoundFont changes.

@{h2}channels
MIDI channels of the MT-32 engine. @{b}std@{ub}: parts on channels 2-10, as a real
MT-32. @{b}alt@{ub}: 1-8 and rhythm on 10, for keyboards that only send on
channel 1 and for music written that way.

@{h2}thru
A USB MIDI keyboard on the Pi also plays the USB MIDI devices attached to it
(see @{" .golem usb " LINK USB}).

@{h2}keys
A USB MIDI keyboard on the Pi also sends what you play to the Next, so
programs there can read it (the Golem plays it as well). Whole messages, no
clock or active sensing.

@{h2}loud
Makes the Golem louder without distortion: its sound is raised and the peaks
are held down by a limiter. Through HDMI the Golem can only be half as loud
as the Next's PCM sound; @{b}loud on@{ub} evens a game's music and effects out.
Very quiet passages come up a little too. Needs firmware golem-1.2.0.

@{h2}How long they last
They survive @{b}play@{ub}, @{b}reset@{ub} and the SysEx a song sends to reset the MT-32.
They last until the Pi is switched off, unless you keep them with
@{" .golem save " LINK SAVE}. Otherwise, at start-up the volume is 100 and fx,
channels and thru come from mt32-pi.cfg on the Pi's SD card. A song may still
change the MT-32 volume itself.

Needs Golem firmware golem-1.1.0 or later; an older one answers
"firmware too old for command".

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node SAVE
@next OUTPUT
@prev SET
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem save

    @{b}.golem save@{ub}
    @{b}.golem save clear@{ub}

@{b}save@{ub} keeps the Golem's current settings for the next time it starts:
engine, ROM set, SoundFont and everything @{" .golem set " LINK SET} and
@{" .golem usb " LINK USB} change. The Pi writes them to golem.cfg, next to
mt32-pi.cfg on its SD card; golem.cfg is read after mt32-pi.cfg and wins.

@{b}save clear@{ub} deletes golem.cfg: the next start uses mt32-pi.cfg alone.

Do not play music while it saves. Needs Golem firmware golem-1.1.0 or later.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node OUTPUT
@next USB
@prev SAVE
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem output

    @{b}.golem output golem@{ub}      Accelerator header (the default)
    @{b}.golem output left@{ub}       Pin 7 of the left joystick port
    @{b}.golem output right@{ub}      Pin 7 of the right joystick port
    @{b}.golem output@{ub}            Report the current output

Chooses where MIDI goes, for every program that uses the driver, until the
driver is uninstalled or the Next restarts. Put it in the autoexec after
@{b}.install@{ub} to keep it.

While a joystick port is the output, it does not work as a joystick (the
other one does). With a joystick output, @{b}.mt32@{ub} and @{b}.gm@{ub} play straight away
without asking the Golem.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node USB
@next RESET
@prev OUTPUT
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem usb

    @{b}.golem usb off@{ub}       Only the Golem plays
    @{b}.golem usb on@{ub}        The Golem and the USB MIDI devices play
    @{b}.golem usb bridge@{ub}    Only the USB MIDI devices; the Golem is silent
    @{b}.golem usb@{ub}           Report the mode and the number of devices

Forwards the Next's MIDI to USB MIDI devices attached to the Pi: a USB-DIN
interface to a real MT-32, or another synth. Needs firmware golem-1.1.0 or
later.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node RESET
@next ALIASES
@prev USB
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.golem reset

    @{b}.golem reset@{ub}

Sends the MT-32 reset and General MIDI System On: clears what earlier songs
loaded with SysEx (instruments, rhythm, effects). @{b}play@{ub} already does it
before each song; use it before playing from another program.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node ALIASES
@next KEYBOARD
@prev RESET
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}.mt32, .gm and .opl3

    @{b}.mt32 play game.mid@{ub}
    @{b}.gm play song.mid@{ub}
    @{b}.opl3 play song.mid@{ub}

They take the same orders as @{b}.golem@{ub}. Before @{b}play@{ub} and @{b}note@{ub} they ask the
Golem for the MT-32, the FluidSynth or the OPL3 engine and wait until it is
ready.
If nothing answers within 0.2 seconds (an external synth), they play anyway.
@{b}.golem@{ub} leaves the engine as it is.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node KEYBOARD
@next ERRORS
@prev ALIASES
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}Playing a MIDI keyboard

A MIDI keyboard on the Pi's USB (directly or through a USB-MIDI interface)
plays the Golem alongside the Next. Both share the 16 channels.

 - FluidSynth: use channel 16, the one GM songs use least. 10 is drums.
 - MT-32: a keyboard on channel 1 needs @{b}.golem set channels alt@{ub}.
 - A dry piano: @{b}.golem set fx off@{ub}.
 - Sound late through the TV: turn on the TV's game mode.
 - For programs on the Next to read the keyboard: @{b}.golem set keys on@{ub}.
   @{b}.midimon@{ub} shows what arrives, in hexadecimal; SPACE ends.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node ERRORS
@next MORE
@prev KEYBOARD
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}Error messages

Errors return to BASIC as error reports, so @{b}ON ERROR@{ub} can handle them.

@{b}GOLEM.DRV missing or incompat.@{ub}
  Install the driver: .install "c:/nextzxos/GOLEM.DRV"

@{b}firmware too old for command@{ub}
  The Pi's firmware lacks this command: update to the latest golem release.

@{b}transport error or timeout@{ub}
  The Pi did not answer: check it runs the Golem firmware and the cable.

@{b}command rejected by Golem@{ub}
  Not available: no ROM or SoundFont with that number, or no SoundFont at all.

@{b}cannot open file@{ub}, @{b}unsupported SMF@{ub}, @{b}invalid SMF event@{ub}
  The MIDI file is missing, has more than 24 tracks or is damaged.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node MORE
@prev ERRORS
@toc MAIN
@{r}golem-next
@{c}@{h1}Where to find more

The full user guide, the programmer's guide and the GSEQ music library are in
the docs folder of the golem-next package:

    github.com/malandante/golem-next

The Golem firmware (GPL-3.0) is at github.com/malandante/mt32-pi.

